Task: canonical token usage (follow-up to 4c7853d)

I asked peer session agui-go-sdk-missing-fields to verify a TypeScript
claim. It verified two things and corrected a third, which is the one that
mattered.

WHAT I GOT WRONG. In 4c7853d I wrote that FR3's "first-party SDKs'
token-usage shape" did not exist. It does. I searched the Go SDK only and
generalised from its absence. The peer checked TS, Python and .NET and
found TokenUsageSchema (events.ts ~283), class TokenUsage
(ag_ui/core/events.py ~50) and AGUI.Abstractions.TokenUsage, carried as a
"usage" array on RUN_FINISHED and RUN_ERROR.

The move off metadata['ag-ui'] still stands: canonical usage is not in
metadata at all, so the spec's location was wrong too, just differently.

WHAT I VERIFIED RATHER THAN ACCEPTED:
- Go SDK really has zero TokenUsage/Usage occurrences in pkg/. Confirmed.
- The peer said RUN_STARTED/FINISHED/ERROR do not declare SubagentRunID.
  My grep found two hits in run_events.go, which looked like a
  contradiction; they are StepStartedEvent and StepFinishedEvent, both
  genuine carriers. Peer was right.
- ADK's mapping: ThoughtsTokenCount and CachedContentTokenCount were being
  dropped and map cleanly onto reasoningTokens and cachedInputTokens.
  ev.Model is NOT promoted through the embedded LLMResponse, so provider
  and model stay absent rather than guessed.

Peer's other two checks, both SAFE, and both correcting a worry of mine:
- BaseEventSchema is .passthrough(), so events tolerate unknown keys. The
  strict-union behaviour applies only to the small outcome objects. My
  concern generalised their RunFinishedOutcome warning too far.
- MetadataSchema is z.record(z.any()), so metadata.adk is accepted and
  preserved verbatim. TS rejects only a null metadata object, which
  omitempty means we never emit.

Design note: kept the array container even though it holds one entry.
Field names alone would leave a structural rewrite for later; the container
costs nothing now.

Still open, and Newton's call: the peer offered to add usage + TokenUsage
to the Go SDK as a third upstream PR, which would let this move to its
proper home on the terminal event.

